[Desktop Entry]
# HACK: overrides the package's launcher entry (same file id in
# ~/.local/share/applications wins over /usr/share/applications). It is a copy
# of /usr/share/applications/omnigent-desktop-electron.desktop with two flags
# added to Exec. Because it shadows the package file entirely, any upstream
# change to Exec/Icon/MimeType is masked: re-diff against the package entry on
# every bump of OMNIGENT_DESKTOP_APP_VERSION in desktop-app.env.
#
# Both flags work around the same fatal check in Chromium's native-Wayland
# backend, CHECK(!bounds.IsEmpty()) in ui::XdgSurface::SetWindowGeometry
# (ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/xdg_surface.cc). Official builds strip the
# message, so the symptom is a silent SIGTRAP of the main process (journal:
# "traps: omnigent-deskto[...] trap int3", systemd-coredump, no stderr). It is
# reachable at all only because Electron >= 38 defaults to native Wayland in a
# Wayland session; XWayland and macOS have no such check. Two distinct triggers
# reach it, observed 2026-09-07 with Omnigent desktop 0.10.0 / Electron 42.7.0
# / Chromium 148.0.7778.280 on sway 1.11 at output scale 1.2:
#
# Trigger 1 (first launch) -> --disable-features=WaylandFractionalScaleV1.
#   With no window_bounds in ~/.config/Omnigent/settings.json Electron centers
#   the window, and on a fractionally scaled output Chromium's fractional-scale
#   path then requests an empty xdg-shell geometry: dead within a second, once.
#   The flag keeps the app on native Wayland with integer buffer scaling.
#   --force-device-scale-factor=1 and WaylandPerSurfaceScale still crashed.
#
# Trigger 2 (any later parent resize/move) -> --ozone-platform=x11.
#   The app's update overlay (web/electron/src/update_overlay.js) is a
#   344x1 frameless child window. When the card is empty, i.e. every launch
#   where "Update ... is not available", the renderer reports height 0 and the
#   shell stores it (heights.set(overlay, 0)), but the parent resize/move
#   handler is
#       const reposition = () => position(parent, overlay, heights.get(overlay) ?? 1);
#   and `??` does not replace 0. So the next time sway retiles the parent, e.g.
#   any window opening on its workspace, the overlay gets setBounds({height: 0});
#   Electron forwards it (visible on the wire as xdg_toplevel.set_max_size(344, 0))
#   and Chromium trips the check. That is why instances died 2.5 s after launch
#   (overlay loads, then sway's first retile), or minutes to hours later on the
#   first retile, and why an instance alone on its workspace survived. Proven by
#   a WAYLAND_DEBUG trace plus an A/B run: an unpacked copy with that line
#   changed to `|| 1` survived forced retiles while the unpatched copy died.
#   Not fixed upstream as of omnigent-ai/omnigent main 3d7dd102 (2026-09-08) or
#   tag v0.12.0; the Linux updater feed still serves 0.10.0. --ozone-platform=x11
#   sidesteps both triggers by running under XWayland (verified: the installed
#   binary survived six forced resize/move commands with it). Cost: XWayland
#   upscaling at scale 1.2 is blurrier than native, and sway now identifies the
#   windows by X11 class/instance "omnigent" instead of app_id (the matchers in
#   bin-linux/sway-windows-lib.sh accept both).
#
# Remove --ozone-platform=x11 once BOTH hold:
#   1. upstream web/electron/src/update_overlay.js no longer repositions with
#      `heights.get(overlay) ?? 1`; the reposition path clamps to >= 1 the way
#      unsuppress() already does (position(parent, overlay, Math.max(1, height)))
#      or the overlay is no longer a separate window at all; and
#   2. that fix is in a Linux build you actually run, i.e. a version newer than
#      the one served by https://omnigent.ai/_desktop/updates/latest-linux.yml
#      at the time of writing (0.10.0), and desktop-app.env is bumped to it.
#   Test (Trigger 2): launch the package Exec plus only the fractional-scale
#   flag with core dumps off, wait ~5 s for the overlay, then
#     swaymsg '[app_id="omnigent" title="^Omnigent$"] resize set height 60 ppt'
#   A window that survives a few resize/move commands means x11 can go;
#   "Trace/breakpoint trap" means keep it.
# Remove --disable-features=WaylandFractionalScaleV1 once Omnigent ships
#   explicit window bounds / this flag itself, or its pinned Electron carries a
#   Chromium where the fractional-scale geometry path no longer produces an
#   empty rect. Test (Trigger 1): delete "window_bounds" from settings.json and
#   run the package Exec with core dumps off:
#     (ulimit -c 0; /opt/Omnigent/omnigent-desktop-electron)
#   A window means the hack is obsolete; "Trace/breakpoint trap" means keep it.
# Both tests are only meaningful while --ozone-platform=x11 is NOT passed.
# Remove this file (and its link in sync.sh) once neither flag is needed.
Name=Omnigent
Exec=/opt/Omnigent/omnigent-desktop-electron --disable-features=WaylandFractionalScaleV1 --ozone-platform=x11 %U
Terminal=false
Type=Application
Icon=omnigent-desktop-electron
StartupWMClass=Omnigent
Comment=Omnigent desktop shell (Electron edition) — a thin native wrapper around the server-served web UI.
MimeType=x-scheme-handler/omnigent;
Categories=Development;
